/bin/zsh -lc "nl -ba packages/web/src/content/docs/skills.mdx | sed -n '1,260p'" in /Users/justbin/project/opensource/opencode
 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	---
     2	title: "Agent Skills"
     3	description: "Define reusable behavior via SKILL.md definitions"
     4	---
     5	
     6	Agent skills let OpenCode discover reusable instructions from your repo or home directory.
     7	Skills are loaded on-demand via the native `skill` tool—agents see available skills and can load the full content when needed.
     8	
     9	---
    10	
    11	## Place files
    12	
    13	Create one folder per skill name and put a `SKILL.md` inside it.
    14	OpenCode searches these locations:
    15	
    16	- Project config: `.opencode/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`
    17	- Global config: `~/.config/opencode/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`
    18	- Project Claude-compatible: `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`
    19	- Global Claude-compatible: `~/.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`
    20	- Project agent-compatible: `.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`
    21	- Global agent-compatible: `~/.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`
    22	
    23	---
    24	
    25	## Understand discovery
    26	
    27	For project-local paths, OpenCode walks up from your current working directory until it reaches the git worktree.
    28	It loads any matching `skills/*/SKILL.md` in `.opencode/` and any matching `.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md` or `.agents/skills/*/SKILL.md` along the way.
    29	
    30	Global definitions are also loaded from `~/.config/opencode/skills/*/SKILL.md`, `~/.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md`, and `~/.agents/skills/*/SKILL.md`.
    31	
    32	---
    33	
    34	## Write frontmatter
    35	
    36	Each `SKILL.md` must start with YAML frontmatter.
    37	Only these fields are recognized:
    38	
    39	- `name` (required)
    40	- `description` (required)
    41	- `license` (optional)
    42	- `compatibility` (optional)
    43	- `metadata` (optional, string-to-string map)
    44	
    45	Unknown frontmatter fields are ignored.
    46	
    47	---
    48	
    49	## Validate names
    50	
    51	`name` must:
    52	
    53	- Be 1–64 characters
    54	- Be lowercase alphanumeric with single hyphen separators
    55	- Not start or end with `-`
    56	- Not contain consecutive `--`
    57	- Match the directory name that contains `SKILL.md`
    58	
    59	Equivalent regex:
    60	
    61	```text
    62	^[a-z0-9]+(-[a-z0-9]+)*$
    63	```
    64	
    65	---
    66	
    67	## Follow length rules
    68	
    69	`description` must be 1-1024 characters.
    70	Keep it specific enough for the agent to choose correctly.
    71	
    72	---
    73	
    74	## Use an example
    75	
    76	Create `.opencode/skills/git-release/SKILL.md` like this:
    77	
    78	```markdown
    79	---
    80	name: git-release
    81	description: Create consistent releases and changelogs
    82	license: MIT
    83	compatibility: opencode
    84	metadata:
    85	  audience: maintainers
    86	  workflow: github
    87	---
    88	
    89	## What I do
    90	
    91	- Draft release notes from merged PRs
    92	- Propose a version bump
    93	- Provide a copy-pasteable `gh release create` command
    94	
    95	## When to use me
    96	
    97	Use this when you are preparing a tagged release.
    98	Ask clarifying questions if the target versioning scheme is unclear.
    99	```
   100	
   101	---
   102	
   103	## Recognize tool description
   104	
   105	OpenCode lists available skills in the `skill` tool description.
   106	Each entry includes the skill name and description:
   107	
   108	```xml
   109	<available_skills>
   110	  <skill>
   111	    <name>git-release</name>
   112	    <description>Create consistent releases and changelogs</description>
   113	  </skill>
   114	</available_skills>
   115	```
   116	
   117	The agent loads a skill by calling the tool:
   118	
   119	```
   120	skill({ name: "git-release" })
   121	```
   122	
   123	---
   124	
   125	## Configure permissions
   126	
   127	Control which skills agents can access using pattern-based permissions in `opencode.json`:
   128	
   129	```json
   130	{
   131	  "permission": {
   132	    "skill": {
   133	      "*": "allow",
   134	      "pr-review": "allow",
   135	      "internal-*": "deny",
   136	      "experimental-*": "ask"
   137	    }
   138	  }
   139	}
   140	```
   141	
   142	| Permission | Behavior                                  |
   143	| ---------- | ----------------------------------------- |
   144	| `allow`    | Skill loads immediately                   |
   145	| `deny`     | Skill hidden from agent, access rejected  |
   146	| `ask`      | User prompted for approval before loading |
   147	
   148	Patterns support wildcards: `internal-*` matches `internal-docs`, `internal-tools`, etc.
   149	
   150	---
   151	
   152	## Override per agent
   153	
   154	Give specific agents different permissions than the global defaults.
   155	
   156	**For custom agents** (in agent frontmatter):
   157	
   158	```yaml
   159	---
   160	permission:
   161	  skill:
   162	    "documents-*": "allow"
   163	---
   164	```
   165	
   166	**For built-in agents** (in `opencode.json`):
   167	
   168	```json
   169	{
   170	  "agent": {
   171	    "plan": {
   172	      "permission": {
   173	        "skill": {
   174	          "internal-*": "allow"
   175	        }
   176	      }
   177	    }
   178	  }
   179	}
   180	```
   181	
   182	---
   183	
   184	## Disable the skill tool
   185	
   186	Completely disable skills for agents that shouldn't use them:
   187	
   188	**For custom agents**:
   189	
   190	```yaml
   191	---
   192	tools:
   193	  skill: false
   194	---
   195	```
   196	
   197	**For built-in agents**:
   198	
   199	```json
   200	{
   201	  "agent": {
   202	    "plan": {
   203	      "tools": {
   204	        "skill": false
   205	      }
   206	    }
   207	  }
   208	}
   209	```
   210	
   211	When disabled, the `<available_skills>` section is omitted entirely.
   212	
   213	---
   214	
   215	## Troubleshoot loading
   216	
   217	If a skill does not show up:
   218	
   219	1. Verify `SKILL.md` is spelled in all caps
   220	2. Check that frontmatter includes `name` and `description`
   221	3. Ensure skill names are unique across all locations
   222	4. Check permissions—skills with `deny` are hidden from agents

