   326	
   327	---
   328	
   329	### Prompt
   330	
   331	Specify a custom system prompt file for this agent with the `prompt` config. The prompt file should contain instructions specific to the agent's purpose.
   332	
   333	```json title="opencode.json"
   334	{
   335	  "agent": {
   336	    "review": {
   337	      "prompt": "{file:./prompts/code-review.txt}"
   338	    }
   339	  }
   340	}
   341	```
   342	
   343	This path is relative to where the config file is located. So this works for both the global OpenCode config and the project specific config.
   344	
   345	---
   346	
   347	### Model
   348	
   349	Use the `model` config to override the model for this agent. Useful for using different models optimized for different tasks. For example, a faster model for planning, a more capable model for implementation.
   350	
   351	:::tip
   352	If you don’t specify a model, primary agents use the [model globally configured](/docs/config#models) while subagents will use the model of the primary agent that invoked the subagent.
   353	:::
   354	
   355	```json title="opencode.json"
   356	{
   357	  "agent": {
   358	    "plan": {
   359	      "model": "anthropic/claude-haiku-4-20250514"
   360	    }
   361	  }
   362	}
   363	```
   364	
   365	The model ID in your OpenCode config uses the format `provider/model-id`. For example, if you're using [OpenCode Zen](/docs/zen), you would use `opencode/gpt-5.1-codex` for GPT 5.1 Codex.
   366	
   367	---
   368	
   369	### Tools (deprecated)
   370	
   371	`tools` is **deprecated**. Prefer the agent's [`permission`](#permissions) field for new configs, updates and more fine-grained control.
   372	
   373	Allows you to control which tools are available in this agent. You can enable or disable specific tools by setting them to `true` or `false`. In an agent's `tools` config, `true` is equivalent to `{"*": "allow"}` permission and `false` is equivalent to `{"*": "deny"}` permission.
   374	
   375	```json title="opencode.json" {3-6,9-12}
   376	{
   377	  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
   378	  "tools": {
   379	    "write": true,
   380	    "bash": true
   381	  },
   382	  "agent": {
   383	    "plan": {
   384	      "tools": {
   385	        "write": false,
   386	        "bash": false
   387	      }
   388	    }
   389	  }
   390	}
   391	```
   392	
   393	:::note
   394	The agent-specific config overrides the global config.
   395	:::
   396	
   397	You can also use wildcards in legacy `tools` entries to control multiple tools at once. For example, to disable all tools from an MCP server:
   398	
   399	```json title="opencode.json"
   400	{
   401	  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
   402	  "agent": {
   403	    "readonly": {
   404	      "tools": {
   405	        "mymcp_*": false,
   406	        "write": false,
   407	        "edit": false
   408	      }
   409	    }
   410	  }
   411	}
   412	```
   413	
   414	[Learn more about tools](/docs/tools).
   415	
   416	---
   417	
   418	### Permissions
   419	
   420	You can configure permissions to manage what actions an agent can take. Currently, the permissions for the `edit`, `bash`, and `webfetch` tools can be configured to:
   421	
   422	- `"ask"` — Prompt for approval before running the tool
   423	- `"allow"` — Allow all operations without approval
   424	- `"deny"` — Disable the tool
   425	
   426	```json title="opencode.json"
   427	{
   428	  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
   429	  "permission": {
   430	    "edit": "deny"
   431	  }
   432	}
   433	```
   434	
   435	You can override these permissions per agent.
   436	
   437	```json title="opencode.json" {3-5,8-10}
   438	{
   439	  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
   440	  "permission": {
   441	    "edit": "deny"
   442	  },
   443	  "agent": {
   444	    "build": {
   445	      "permission": {
   446	        "edit": "ask"
   447	      }
   448	    }
   449	  }
   450	}
   451	```
   452	
   453	You can also set permissions in Markdown agents.
   454	
   455	```markdown title="~/.config/opencode/agents/review.md"
   456	---
   457	description: Code review without edits
   458	mode: subagent
   459	permission:
   460	  edit: deny
   461	  bash:
   462	    "*": ask
   463	    "git diff": allow
   464	    "git log*": allow
   465	    "grep *": allow
   466	  webfetch: deny
   467	---
   468	
   469	Only analyze code and suggest changes.
   470	```
   471	
   472	You can set permissions for specific bash commands.
   473	
   474	```json title="opencode.json" {7}
   475	{
   476	  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
   477	  "agent": {
   478	    "build": {
   479	      "permission": {
   480	        "bash": {
   481	          "git push": "ask",
   482	          "grep *": "allow"
   483	        }
   484	      }
   485	    }
   486	  }
   487	}
   488	```
   489	
   490	This can take a glob pattern.
   491	
   492	```json title="opencode.json" {7}
   493	{
   494	  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
   495	  "agent": {
   496	    "build": {
   497	      "permission": {
   498	        "bash": {
   499	          "git *": "ask"
   500	        }
   501	      }
   502	    }
   503	  }
   504	}
   505	```
   506	
   507	And you can also use the `*` wildcard to manage permissions for all commands.
   508	Since the last matching rule takes precedence, put the `*` wildcard first and specific rules after.
   509	
   510	```json title="opencode.json" {8}
   511	{
   512	  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
   513	  "agent": {
   514	    "build": {
   515	      "permission": {
   516	        "bash": {
   517	          "*": "ask",
   518	          "git status *": "allow"
   519	        }
   520	      }
   521	    }
   522	  }
   523	}
   524	```
   525	
   526	[Learn more about permissions](/docs/permissions).
   527	
   528	---
   529	
   530	### Mode
   531	
   532	Control the agent's mode with the `mode` config. The `mode` option is used to determine how the agent can be used.
   533	
   534	```json title="opencode.json"
   535	{
   536	  "agent": {
   537	    "review": {
   538	      "mode": "subagent"
   539	    }
   540	  }
   541	}
   542	```
   543	
   544	The `mode` option can be set to `primary`, `subagent`, or `all`. If no `mode` is specified, it defaults to `all`.
   545	
   546	---
   547	
   548	### Hidden
   549	
   550	Hide a subagent from the `@` autocomplete menu with `hidden: true`. Useful for internal subagents that should only be invoked programmatically by other agents via the Task tool.
   551	
   552	```json title="opencode.json"
   553	{
   554	  "agent": {
   555	    "internal-helper": {
   556	      "mode": "subagent",
   557	      "hidden": true
   558	    }
   559	  }
